// test: fresh return
// expect-exit: 0
// expect-output:
// sum 889000
// chain 889000
// maybe 2000
// boxes 1498500
// freed True
// collections 0
// grown 2017 2015
// kept 777 777 777
// held 42
//
// A helper that makes an object and returns it hands it over: the caller
// owns it and frees it at its last use (Escape's fresh-return rule). Every
// helper here is padded past the inliner's limits and called from two
// places, so it is a real call when escape analysis runs and only the
// summary can decide. Chains of helpers compose; a helper that may return
// null is still fresh. Two objects must NOT be freed: one the helper also
// keeps in a static, and one the caller keeps; both are read back after
// thousands of frees would have reused their memory. And `x = Grow(x)`
// reads the old object inside the call that makes the new one, so the old
// one may only be given back after the call. Up to the check, nothing
// needs the collector: every object is freed by the compiler. The grow loop
// after it is checked for its values: its result goes into a variable the
// loop also assigns, which the owned-variables rule (674) takes over.

class Box
{
    public long V;
    public long W;
}

